  "tiles": [
    {
      "tile_id": "tile_data_shape_and_types",
      "title": "Datasets and Variable Types",
      "summary": "What a dataset looks like (rows are observations, columns are variables) and what kind of values a column holds (categorical or numerical).",
      "steps": [
        {
          "step_id": "tile_data_shape_and_types.s01",
          "type": "explainer",
          "concept_ids": [
            "data.dataset_rows_columns"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "DATASETS AND VARIABLE TYPES",
          "title": "A dataset is a collection of observations",
          "lede": "Northstar Store wants to understand its orders better, so it starts by looking at how the data is laid out.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "text": "Northstar records every delivery the same way: an order number, the city it went to, which driver handled it and how long it took. All those orders, each written down with the same set of details, form what analysts call a dataset -- a collection of observations recorded on the same set of variables.",
              "role": "intro",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#tb1"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#f1"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "data_table",
              "asset_ref": {
                "asset_id": "northstar_orders",
                "variant_id": null,
                "columns": [
                  "order_id",
                  "customer_segment",
                  "city",
                  "driver",
                  "items",
                  "order_value_ngn",
                  "delivery_minutes"
                ]
              },
              "caption": "Northstar's delivery orders, one order per line.",
              "inspect": {
                "enabled": true,
                "caption_template": "For order {order_id}, {column} = {value}.",
                "id_column": "order_id"
              },
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns"
                ],
                "anchors": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#visual"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "text": "Analysts most often keep a dataset as a table -- sometimes called a data matrix -- made of rows and columns: a grid where every order has its own line and every detail about an order has its own column.",
              "role": "definition",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#tb2"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#f2"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "data.dataset_rows_columns"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "data.dataset_rows_columns#tb1",
              "data.dataset_rows_columns#tb2"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "data.dataset_rows_columns#f1",
              "data.dataset_rows_columns#f2"
            ],
            "anchors": [
              "data.dataset_rows_columns#visual"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "step_id": "tile_data_shape_and_types.s02",
          "type": "explainer",
          "concept_ids": [
            "data.dataset_rows_columns"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "DATASETS AND VARIABLE TYPES",
          "title": "Rows and columns mean different things",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "Every row is one observation",
              "level": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "text": "Each row represents one observation -- one record. In Northstar's table that means one row per order: NS1001 is one row, NS1002 is the next, and each is a separate delivery.",
              "role": "mechanism",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#tb3"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#f3"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "Every column is one variable",
              "level": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "text": "Each column represents one variable: one attribute recorded for every observation. The city column holds the destination city for every order, and delivery_minutes holds the delivery time for every order -- each column holds just one kind of information.",
              "role": "mechanism",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#tb3"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#f4"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "text": "That tells you how to read the table. Asked \"how many deliveries went to Lagos?\", you scan down the city column and count the matching rows. Asked \"what happened with order NS1004?\", you read across that one row. A row tells you about one delivery; a column tells you about one kind of fact across all deliveries.",
              "role": "example",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#tb4"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#f3",
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#f4"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "data.dataset_rows_columns"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "data.dataset_rows_columns#tb3",
              "data.dataset_rows_columns#tb4"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "data.dataset_rows_columns#f3",
              "data.dataset_rows_columns#f4"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "step_id": "tile_data_shape_and_types.s03",
          "type": "check",
          "concept_ids": [
            "data.dataset_rows_columns"
          ],
          "title": "Check: what a column holds",
          "reward": {
            "gems": 5
          },
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "data.dataset_rows_columns"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "data.dataset_rows_columns#f4"
            ],
            "anchors": [
              "data.dataset_rows_columns#assessment:identify_column_as_variable"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "step_id": "tile_data_shape_and_types.s04",
          "type": "hands_on",
          "concept_ids": [
            "data.dataset_rows_columns"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "HANDS-ON",
          "title": "Read the dataset",
          "lede": "Use the Northstar table to answer three quick tasks.",
          "reward": {
            "gems": 10
          },
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "data.dataset_rows_columns"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "data.dataset_rows_columns#f1",
              "data.dataset_rows_columns#f2",
              "data.dataset_rows_columns#f3",
              "data.dataset_rows_columns#f4"
            ],
            "anchors": [
              "data.dataset_rows_columns#practice",
              "data.dataset_rows_columns#assessment:describe_dataset_structure",
              "data.dataset_rows_columns#assessment:identify_row_as_observation",
              "data.dataset_rows_columns#assessment:identify_column_as_variable"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "step_id": "tile_data_shape_and_types.s05",
          "type": "explainer",
          "concept_ids": [
            "data.categorical_numeric_types"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "DATASETS AND VARIABLE TYPES",
          "title": "Not every column means the same kind of thing",
          "lede": "Some variables describe groups or labels. Others describe amounts we can measure or count.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "text": "Both city and delivery_minutes are columns in the same table, but they behave completely differently. Knowing which kind of column you are looking at changes what you are allowed to do with it.",
              "role": "intro",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types#tb1"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types#f1",
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types#f2"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "data_table",
              "asset_ref": {
                "asset_id": "northstar_orders",
                "variant_id": null,
                "columns": [
                  "order_id",
                  "city",
                  "zone_code",
                  "items",
                  "order_value_ngn",
                  "delivery_minutes"
                ]
              },
              "caption": "The same orders, with each column tagged by kind.",
              "tag_columns": [
                {
                  "column": "city",
                  "tag": "categorical",
                  "tone": "neutral"
                },
                {
                  "column": "zone_code",
                  "tag": "categorical",
                  "tone": "focus"
                },
                {
                  "column": "items",
                  "tag": "numerical",
                  "tone": "good"
                },
                {
                  "column": "order_value_ngn",
                  "tag": "numerical",
                  "tone": "good"
                },
                {
                  "column": "delivery_minutes",
                  "tag": "numerical",
                  "tone": "good"
                }
              ],
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types"
                ],
                "anchors": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types#visual"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "Numerical variables",
              "level": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "text": "A numerical variable takes numerical values for which it is sensible to add or subtract. delivery_minutes is numerical: 35 minutes plus 41 minutes, or the difference between two delivery times, both mean something real.",
              "role": "definition",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types#tb2"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types#f1"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "Categorical variables",
              "level": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "text": "A categorical variable's values are categories, also called levels. city is categorical: its values -- Lagos, Abuja, Kano -- are labels for groups, and there is nothing to add or subtract between them.",
              "role": "definition",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types#tb2"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types#f2"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "tone": "misconception",
              "text": "A column stored as numbers is not automatically numerical. zone_code holds 101, 102, 103 -- but adding two zone codes gives nothing useful, just as adding two telephone area codes would. It is a label, not a quantity, so it is categorical even though it is written as digits.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types#tb3"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types#m1"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "data.categorical_numeric_types"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#tb1",
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#tb2",
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#tb3"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#f1",
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#f2",
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#m1"
            ],
            "anchors": [
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#visual"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "step_id": "tile_data_shape_and_types.s06",
          "type": "check",
          "concept_ids": [
            "data.categorical_numeric_types"
          ],
          "title": "Check: spot the numerical column",
          "reward": {
            "gems": 5
          },
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "data.categorical_numeric_types"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#f1",
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#f2"
            ],
            "anchors": [
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#assessment:classify_variable_type"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "step_id": "tile_data_shape_and_types.s07",
          "type": "hands_on",
          "concept_ids": [
            "data.categorical_numeric_types"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "HANDS-ON",
          "title": "Sort the data",
          "lede": "Sort each value from Northstar's table into the group it belongs to.",
          "reward": {
            "gems": 10
          },
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "data.categorical_numeric_types"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#f1",
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#f2",
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#m1"
            ],
            "anchors": [
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#practice",
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#assessment:recognize_numeric_looking_categorical"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "step_id": "tile_data_shape_and_types.s08",
          "type": "recap",
          "concept_ids": [
            "data.dataset_rows_columns",
            "data.categorical_numeric_types"
          ],
          "title": "What you can do now",
          "reward": {
            "gems": 5
          },
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "text": "A row is one observation and a column is one variable. Before you check, clean or make sense of Northstar's data, you need to know that a row is one order and a column is one attribute of every order.",
              "role": "takeaway",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#tb5"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#f1",
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#f2",
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#f3",
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#f4"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "text": "Before doing anything with a column, check which kind it is: can its values be meaningfully added or subtracted, or are they just labels for groups? That decides whether treating the column as a quantity will make sense at all.",
              "role": "takeaway",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types#tb4"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types#f1",
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types#f2"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "up_next",
              "title": "Basic data-quality checks",
              "text": "Next: use Northstar's messy table to decide whether the data can be trusted."
            }
          ],
          "lineage": {
